  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday Night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1144 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nA hot and humid, persistent forecast will be the main takeaway \nthrough at least the remainder of the workweek, perhaps even \nbeyond. Expect daily afternoon temperatures maxing out in the \nupper 90s and lower 100s, with heat indices hovering between 105 \nand 110 F, each afternoon. A Heat Advisory remains in effect for \nthe entire forecast area from now through 8 PM Friday. \n\nThe incessant ridge will hold steady over the \nsouthern/southeastern CONUS through the remainder of the week and \ninto this upcoming weekend, keeping the jet stream and any\nsignificant rain chances well north of the area through the long-\nterm. The ridge will gradually restrengthen over the \nMississippi/Tennessee Valleys, with models showing a 598-600 \ndecameter ridge by Friday evening/night. Guidance then shows the \ncenter of the ridge retrograding westward and overhead late in \nthe weekend and into early next week. In theory, this would likely\nbe some of the hottest days of the year, but this will also be \nhighly dependent on exact position and strength of the ridge as it\nmoves overhead. Medium-range models continue suggesting a weak \nquasi-stationary frontal boundary, that will be draped across the\nCentral Plains late in the week, will sag south and push into far\nnortheast OK late Sunday night and into Monday morning, with the \nfront pushing farther south through the day Monday. However, \ntrends in the forecast models over the last few days of this front\nhave been unreliable and inconsistent. Will have better details \nand a better grasp of how this evolves by the end of the week. For\nnow, the oppressive heat looks to continue through the long-term \nperiod. \n\nMejia",
